          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line drug therapy for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Metformin is the usual first drug when there is no other major condition that changes the choice. Current guidance, as described in the National diabetes association standards of care summary (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy), says the first drug now depends on other conditions the person has. It is no longer a fixed \"metformin first\" sequence.\n\n- **Without a compelling related condition:** Metformin, alongside structured lifestyle changes, is the usual starting drug. The document gives its advantages as long experience, low cost, no weight gain, and little risk of low blood sugar on its own. Kidney function is checked before starting and periodically afterward.\n- **With other conditions:** The document says the choice can be made separately from the HbA1c blood test result. The passage I received does not list which conditions these are, so I can't describe them from these sources. Ask a clinician, or see the full guideline.\n\n**Differences between authorities:** My search did not find a disagreement about first-line diabetes drugs. The disagreement sections it returned covered hypertension and lipid targets, not diabetes. I also could not retrieve the NICE (UK) guidance, so I can't say whether it matches or differs. Treat the picture as incomplete.\n\n**Evidence strength:** The retrieved summary states what guidance recommends. It does not state how strong the underlying trial evidence is, so I can't rate that here.\n\nIf you have a specific situation, such as kidney disease or heart problems, a clinician can apply this guidance to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
